\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Downstream benchmarks (Qwen3-0.6B, $\rho{=}0.4$, seed\,42).
All differences within standard error; adaptive reweighting preserves downstream performance.}
\label{tab:downstream}
\end{table}

\section{Higher Contamination Details}
\label{app:rho06}

Table~\ref{tab:rho06} reports per-seed results at $\rho{=}0.6$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Seed 42} & \textbf{Seed 123} & \textbf{Seed 456} \\
\midrule
Adaptive $\bstar$ & 17.740 & 17.732 & 17.735 \\
Uniform           & 17.766 & 17.754 & 17.770 \\
$\Delta$          & $-$0.026 & $-$0.022 & $-$0.035 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{@{}l@{}}{Mean: $17.736 \pm 0.004$ vs.\ $17.763 \pm 0.008$ ($\Delta{=}{-}0.028 \pm 0.007$, $p{<}0.01$)} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Per-seed results at $\rho{=}0.6$ (Qwen3-0.6B, $\tau{=}0.8$, $\nmin{=}2$).}
\label{tab:rho06}
\end{table}

Degradation rates from $\rho{=}0.4$ to $\rho{=}0.6$ (seed\,42):
adaptive $+$0.087, uniform $+$0.072, grid $b{=}0.5$ $+$0.053, ESS-only $+$0.040.
The fixed exponent $b{=}0.5$ degrades least among effective methods, consistent with $\tau{=}0.8$ being slightly aggressive at higher contamination ($\tau{=}0.7$ optimal at $\rho{=}0.6$).
The ESS-only/uniform gap collapses from 0.043 at $\rho{=}0.4$ to 0.004 at $\rho{=}0.6$ (seed\,42: 17.770 vs.\ 17.766); ESS binding rises to ${\sim}$66\% of batches, leaving insufficient room for $b$ to effectively suppress synthetic samples.
The adaptive method maintains its advantage through the $\aeff$ quality constraint, which operates independently of the ESS ceiling.

